<div class="legal-page">
  <h1 class="page-title">Is this recruiter real?</h1>
  <p class="updated-date">Check an email address before you reply, click or share anything.</p>

  <div class="content-card">
    <h2>Check a recruiter's email</h2>
    <p>Paste the email address the recruiter used. We'll look at how long their email domain has existed, whether it imitates a well-known company, whether it can receive email, and whether the address belongs to a verified recruiter on PreCheckd.</p>
    <form class="pc-form" id="public-check-form">
      <input type="email" id="public-check-input" placeholder="name@company.com" autocomplete="off" required>
      <button type="submit" id="public-check-btn" class="btn btn-primary">Check</button>
    </form>
    <div class="pc-results" id="public-check-results" aria-live="polite"></div>
    <p class="pc-note">These are signals, not a verdict. A new domain or a free email address doesn't prove a scam, and an established one doesn't prove a recruiter is honest. We don't save the address you check.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-card">
    <h2>Signs of a fake recruiter</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>They ask for money.</strong> Real employers don't ask you to pay for equipment, training, background checks or software, and they don't send a check and ask you to send some back.</li>
      <li><strong>The interview is only by chat.</strong> Interviews held entirely over Telegram, WhatsApp or text, with no call or video, are a common scam pattern.</li>
      <li><strong>An offer with no real interview.</strong> Being hired within a day or two, with no one asking about your experience, is a red flag.</li>
      <li><strong>The email doesn't match the company.</strong> A free address (like Gmail), or a domain that is almost the company's name but not quite, like <em>amaz0n-careers.com</em> or <em>microsoft-hiring.net</em>, deserves a closer look. The checker above flags names that imitate well-known employers.</li>
      <li><strong>The email domain is brand new.</strong> Scam domains are often only days or weeks old. The checker shows how old the domain is.</li>
      <li><strong>The domain can't receive email, or has no website.</strong> A real company's address can be replied to and the company has a website. The checker looks for both.</li>
      <li><strong>They want sensitive details early.</strong> A Social Security number, bank details or a copy of your ID before any real hiring step is a warning sign.</li>
      <li><strong>Pressure and secrecy.</strong> "Reply today," "don't tell anyone," or a job that sounds too good for the work involved.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to confirm a recruiter yourself</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Find the company's own website and careers page, and check that the job is listed there.</li>
      <li>Call the company's main number from its website, not a number in the email, and ask whether the recruiter works there.</li>
      <li>Look the recruiter up on LinkedIn and see whether their profile is real, has history and matches the company.</li>
      <li>Never click a link or open an attachment until you're confident the sender is real.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>If you've been scammed or targeted</h2>
    <p>You can report it to the FTC at <a href="https://reportfraud.ftc.gov" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">reportfraud.ftc.gov</a> and to the FBI's Internet Crime Complaint Center at <a href="https://www.ic3.gov" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">ic3.gov</a>. If you've sent money or bank details, contact your bank right away. You can also <a href="/fraud">report the sender to PreCheckd</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-card">
    <h2>Why PreCheckd exists</h2>
    <p>On PreCheckd, every recruiter is verified before they can contact a candidate: identity, phone, email and company. That way you know who is on the other end before you share anything.</p>
    <p><a href="/recruiter-search" class="btn btn-primary">Browse verified recruiters</a> &nbsp; <a href="/candidate-signup">Create a free candidate profile</a></p>
  </div>
